    summary: 'PCIe connects every device in the host to the CPU through a tree of root ports and switches. Bandwidth scales with lanes and generation: a Gen4 x16 link is nominally ≈ 32 GB/s per direction (≈ 64 GB/s bidirectional), Gen5 x16 ≈ 64 GB/s per direction, Gen6 x16 ≈ 128 GB/s (example figures, before protocol overhead). Latency for a round trip is on the order of a microsecond, orders of magnitude above DRAM. It bounds host-to-device copies, GPU-to-NIC traffic and any GPU-to-GPU traffic that has no NVLink path.',
    purpose: 'Provides the general-purpose, memory-mapped I/O fabric of the host; everything the GPU exchanges with the CPU, disks and network crosses it unless a dedicated fabric (NVLink) exists.',
    mechanics: 'Each link is `N` lanes of full-duplex serial signalling; Gen3 runs 8 GT/s per lane with 128b/130b encoding (≈ 0.985 GB/s per lane per direction), Gen4 16 GT/s, Gen5 32 GT/s, Gen6 64 GT/s with PAM4 and FLIT mode. Data moves in transaction layer packets (TLPs) of up to the negotiated max payload (commonly 256 B, sometimes 512 B), each with 12-16 B of headers plus link-layer framing, so achievable bandwidth is roughly 85-92% of nominal for large transfers and much less for small ones. Reads are split transactions: a requester posts a read and waits for completion TLPs, so read latency (≈ 0.5-1 µs across a root port, more through switches) and the number of outstanding requests bound read throughput; writes are posted and pipeline better, which is why DMA engines push rather than pull where possible. Topology is a tree: the CPU root complex has a fixed number of lanes (for example 128 Gen5 lanes per socket on recent server CPUs, generation dependent), fanned out through PCIe switches to GPUs, NICs and NVMe; two devices under the same switch can exchange peer-to-peer TLPs without touching the CPU, while devices under different root ports traverse the root complex and, across sockets, the inter-socket link (UPI/Infinity Fabric), which usually lowers P2P bandwidth and raises latency. Access Control Services (ACS) on switches can force P2P traffic up to the root complex for IOMMU checking, which is a common cause of low GPU-to-GPU or GPU-to-NIC bandwidth. Devices expose memory through BARs; the GPU\'s BAR1 window maps device memory so the CPU or other devices can address it (resizable BAR makes the whole HBM addressable), which GPUDirect relies on. Link width and speed are negotiated at boot and can silently train down (x8 instead of x16, Gen3 instead of Gen4) on marginal risers or after errors; `lspci -vv` shows `LnkSta` and `nvidia-smi -q` shows current and max link width and generation.',
