    summary: 'A host-to-device copy is a DMA by a GPU copy engine reading host memory over PCIe and writing HBM. The DMA engine needs a fixed physical address, so only pinned (page-locked) memory can be copied directly and asynchronously; pageable memory is first copied by the CPU into a driver-owned pinned staging buffer in chunks, which costs CPU time, cannot fully overlap and makes the call effectively synchronous with respect to the host buffer. Large pinned copies reach most of PCIe bandwidth; small copies are dominated by fixed per-copy latency.',
    purpose: 'Moves inputs, sampled tokens, offloaded KV blocks and weights across the PCIe boundary with as little CPU involvement and as much overlap with compute as possible.',
    mechanics: '`cudaMemcpyAsync(dst, src, n, kind, stream)` enqueues a copy command on the stream; if `src` is pinned the copy engine executes it in stream order while the CPU continues, and completion is observed with an event or a synchronize. With pageable `src`, the runtime memcpys chunks (on the order of 64 KB to a few MB, driver dependent) into a pinned staging area and issues DMA per chunk; the call returns only after the source has been consumed, throughput drops to whatever the CPU copy sustains, and the copy occupies the calling thread. Pinning via `cudaMallocHost`/`cudaHostAlloc` or `cudaHostRegister` (PyTorch `pin_memory=True`, DataLoader `pin_memory`) locks the pages so the kernel cannot swap or move them; pinning is itself a slow driver operation (page table walks, IOMMU mapping), so buffers are pinned once and reused, and pinning many GB reduces memory the OS can page. Achievable H2D on Gen4 x16 is roughly 25-27 GB/s and on Gen5 x16 roughly 50-55 GB/s for multi-MB pinned copies (example); a copy of a few KB costs ~10 µs end to end regardless of size, so a thousand small copies per step cost milliseconds. Copies on a stream overlap with kernels on other streams because copy engines are independent of the SMs; GPUs have separate engines for each direction, so H2D and D2H overlap too. `tensor.to(device, non_blocking=True)` from a pinned tensor is the PyTorch form; from a pageable tensor the flag is honoured only for the enqueue, not for overlap. Decode needs one D2H per step for the sampled token ids (batch × 4 bytes, tiny) but that copy is followed by a synchronization before the CPU can build the next input, so its latency (PCIe round trip plus event wait, several microseconds to tens) sits directly on the per-token critical path; engines pipeline the next step or keep the sampling loop on the GPU to hide it. `tensor.item()` and `.cpu()` on pageable memory are the implicit synchronous D2H that stalls the launch pipeline. Unified memory and `cudaMemcpyPeer` follow the same DMA rules; mapped pinned memory (zero-copy) lets kernels read host memory over PCIe directly at PCIe bandwidth.',
