    summary: 'An all-reduce of `S` bytes across `n` GPUs in a ring sends and receives `2·(n−1)/n · S` bytes per GPU in `2·(n−1)` steps, so time ≈ `2(n−1)/n · S / bw + 2(n−1) · latency`. Large messages are bandwidth-bound and the ring is near optimal; small messages are latency-bound and a tree (log n steps) or a low-latency protocol wins. NCCL picks algorithm (Ring, Tree, CollNet/SHARP, NVLS on NVSwitch) and protocol (Simple, LL, LL128) per collective and size based on the topology it discovered.',
    purpose: 'Predicts and bounds the communication cost of TP, DP and EP so parallelism and batch decisions can be made against measured link bandwidth rather than hope.',
    mechanics: 'Ring all-reduce = reduce-scatter (each GPU ends with one fully reduced `S/n` chunk after `n−1` steps) followed by all-gather (`n−1` steps); per step each GPU sends `S/n` to its successor and receives from its predecessor, so total bytes per GPU are `2·(n−1)·S/n` and every link is busy in every step, which is why ring achieves near full link bandwidth for large `S`. Its latency term grows linearly in `n`; tree all-reduce (reduce up, broadcast down, or NCCL\'s double binary tree) has `O(log n)` steps and lower latency but lower per-link utilization, so NCCL uses tree for small sizes and ring for large, with a crossover it tunes from its topology model. NVLS (NVSwitch multicast and in-switch reduction, Hopper) and CollNet/SHARP (in-network reduction on InfiniBand switches) offload the reduction into the fabric and roughly halve the bytes each GPU must move. Protocols: Simple moves large chunks with a flag after each, LL packs 8-byte data+flag words so the receiver polls without a separate synchronization (lowest latency, ≈ 50% bandwidth efficiency), LL128 uses 128-byte lines with a flag per line (near full bandwidth on NVLink, needs ordered 128-byte writes so it is disabled on some PCIe paths). nccl-tests report `algbw = S / time` and `busbw = algbw × 2(n−1)/n` for all-reduce; busbw is the number to compare with link bandwidth (for example ≈ 400-480 GB/s per direction achievable on an 8×H100 NVSwitch node, example, reported). Bandwidth-bound regime: `S / bw` dominates (gradient all-reduce of GB-sized buckets, all-gather of sharded weights); latency-bound regime: a TP all-reduce in decode moves `batch × hidden × 2 bytes`, for example 8 × 8192 × 2 = 128 KB, which at 900 GB/s would take 0.15 µs but actually costs ≈ 10-20 µs of fixed latency, twice per layer, so at batch 1-8 tensor parallelism is paid in latency floors, not bandwidth. Overlap: NCCL runs on its own streams and its kernels occupy a few SMs; DP gradient all-reduce is bucketed and launched as backward proceeds; TP overlap needs the GEMM split into chunks so the all-reduce of chunk `k` runs during GEMM chunk `k+1` (sequence-parallel or async TP variants). NCCL needs `NCCL_NET_GDR_LEVEL`/`NCCL_IB_HCA` for RDMA and prints its chosen algorithms at `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO`.',
