    keywords: ['ibv_reg_mr', 'ibv_post_send', 'RDMA_WRITE / RDMA_READ', 'completion queue', 'rkey / lkey', 'PFC', 'ECN / DCQCN', 'UCX', 'libfabric', 'NIXL'],
    summary: 'RDMA lets a NIC read from or write to registered memory on a remote node directly, with no kernel involvement or CPU copy on either side after setup. InfiniBand is a native lossless fabric with credit-based flow control; RoCE v2 carries the same verbs over UDP/IP on Ethernet and depends on PFC and ECN to stay lossless. Latency is around 1-2 µs for small messages, bandwidth is the NIC rate, and the CPU cost per byte is near zero, which is why NCCL, GPUDirect and disaggregated-serving KV transfer are built on it.',
    purpose: 'Provides the lowest-latency, lowest-CPU path for bulk data between nodes, making multi-node training collectives and cross-node KV movement feasible at line rate.',
    mechanics: 'Setup: each side registers memory (`ibv_reg_mr`), which pins the pages, programs the NIC\'s translation table and returns a local key (`lkey`) and a remote key (`rkey`) that authorizes remote access; registration is slow (microseconds to milliseconds per region, proportional to size) so regions are registered once and cached. Endpoints are queue pairs (send and receive queues, typically Reliable Connected) plus completion queues; work requests are posted to the queues from user space by writing descriptors and ringing a doorbell (an MMIO write), and completions are polled or signalled. Two-sided operations (send/recv) require the receiver to pre-post buffers; one-sided operations (RDMA write, RDMA read, atomics) target a remote address and rkey and complete without the remote CPU seeing anything, so a decode node can pull KV blocks from a prefill node\'s GPU memory with a single RDMA read once addresses are exchanged. Message size matters: a 4 KB write is latency-bound (≈ 1-2 µs), while multi-MB transfers reach ≈ 90% of link rate; many in-flight requests across multiple QPs are needed to fill a 400 Gb/s link. Lossless fabric: InfiniBand uses link-level credits; RoCE v2 (UDP port 4791) needs Priority Flow Control on the RDMA traffic class plus ECN-based congestion control (DCQCN) so the NIC never sees drops that would trigger slow go-back-N recovery; misconfigured PFC produces either loss or pause storms and head-of-line blocking. Inter-node NCCL uses verbs directly (`NCCL_IB_HCA` selects devices), UCX and libfabric wrap verbs for frameworks, and KV-transfer libraries in inference engines (NIXL, Mooncake transfer engine, example) use one-sided RDMA between GPU buffers with GPUDirect. Registration of GPU memory requires GPUDirect RDMA support (nvidia-peermem or dma-buf); otherwise data is staged through host memory. Tools: `ibv_devinfo`, `ib_write_bw`/`ib_write_lat` (perftest), `rdma resource show`, `ibdiagnet`, per-port counters for retransmits and pause frames.',
    input: 'Registered memory regions with keys, queue pairs connected between peers, work requests (address, length, key, opcode).',
    output: 'Bytes landed in the remote (or local) registered memory and a completion entry; no intermediate copies.',
    tensorShape: 'MR = (addr, length, lkey, rkey) over pinned pages; QP = send queue + receive queue + CQ; WQE ≈ 64 B descriptor.',
    memory: 'Registered regions are pinned for their lifetime (host or GPU memory); a KV pool for transfer is registered once at start-up so per-request transfers cost no registration.',
    compute: 'Near-zero CPU per byte; polling completion queues costs a core if latency matters, interrupt-driven completion trades latency for CPU.',
    communication: 'Bandwidth = NIC rate (≈ 50 GB/s per direction for 400 Gb/s, example) per NIC; latency ≈ 1-2 µs plus fabric hops; requires the same lossless configuration end to end.',
    knobs: ['InfiniBand vs RoCE v2 (and PFC/ECN configuration on RoCE)', 'Number of QPs and outstanding work requests per connection', 'Registration caching / pre-registered pools', 'Message chunk size for large transfers', 'GPUDirect vs host-staged transfers', 'Traffic class and QoS separation of RDMA from TCP traffic'],
    metrics: ['ib_write_bw / ib_read_lat baselines per pair', 'Port counters: bytes, retransmits, out-of-sequence, pause frames', 'NCCL inter-node busbw vs NIC rate', 'KV transfer time per request and registration cache hit rate in the engine', 'Completion queue polling CPU usage'],
    failureModes: ['RoCE without correctly configured PFC/ECN causing drops and catastrophic slowdowns', 'Registering memory per transfer instead of once', 'Single QP unable to saturate a 400 Gb/s link', 'GPU memory not registrable (no peermem/dma-buf) silently falling back to host staging', 'NCCL choosing the wrong HCA (management network) because NCCL_IB_HCA was unset', 'Pinned RDMA buffers exhausting lockable memory limits (ulimit -l)'],
